 <h1 style="margin-top:24px;">Acquiring a Collector Automobile: The Private Path to Authenticity and Discretion</h1>
 <p style="color:var(--gold); font-size:0.8rem; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:1.5em;">Published 4 August 2026</p>
 <p>There's a real difference between ordering an automobile and commissioning one — in process, in access, and in the guarantees involved. This guide covers what to verify before committing capital to a rare automobile or a bespoke configuration, and why the private route reduces risk compared with the open market.</p>
@@ -81,7 +81,7 @@ gtag('config', 'G-S1Q6BFKL8L');
 <p>The value of a rare automobile can't be read off a price list. It depends on provenance, the rarity of that specific configuration, condition relative to original specification, and market timing for that particular model — which can move independently of the broader collector market. An independent valuation, not tied to the seller, remains the most reliable way to judge whether the asking price reflects the real market.</p>
 <h2>The role of a private intermediary</h2>
 <p>Orion supports clients through provenance verification, discreet negotiation, and, when requested, access to bespoke configuration channels at manufacturers. We operate on a success-fee basis: our compensation depends on the transaction closing successfully, not on the volume of deals suggested.</p>
