 <h1 style="margin-top:24px;">Buying Luxury Real Estate in Dubai: A Guide for International Investors</h1>
 <p style="color:var(--gold); font-size:0.8rem; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:1.5em;">Published 4 August 2026</p>
 <p>Dubai continues to draw private capital toward its premium real estate sector — not on trend, but on a precise combination of taxation, regulatory stability, and the quality of its residential offering. This guide covers what to know before committing capital to the Emirates, and where a private intermediary reduces the risk along the way.</p>
@@ -81,7 +81,7 @@ gtag('config', 'G-S1Q6BFKL8L');
 <p>The most concrete risk for an international investor isn't Dubai itself — it's transacting remotely, without direct visibility into the developer, the actual state of construction, or the genuine availability of a unit marketed as exclusive. Verifying the Dubai Land Department registry, the developer's delivery history on prior projects, and the real escrow protection of payments made are steps that require presence and relationships on the ground — not a well-built website.</p>
 <h2>The role of a private intermediary</h2>
 <p>Orion introduces clients to verified real estate opportunities in Dubai before they reach broad distribution, and conducts preliminary verification — developer, project status, payment structure — before a client commits capital. Our model runs on success fees: we're compensated only when the transaction closes, which aligns our interest with the client's from the first conversation, not just at signing.</p>
