 <h1 style="margin-top:24px;">Luxury Timepieces: A Guide to Acquisition and Discreet Sale of a Collection</h1>
 <p style="color:var(--gold); font-size:0.8rem; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:1.5em;">Published 4 August 2026</p>
 <p>The high-end watch market combines two dynamics rarely compatible elsewhere: scarcity managed directly by the maisons, and a secondary market that's active and often more liquid than the primary one. Understanding how to move between the two is the difference between paying the right price and paying for someone else's wait.</p>
@@ -79,7 +79,7 @@ gtag('config', 'G-S1Q6BFKL8L');
 <p>A high-value watch needs a dedicated policy, separate from home contents coverage — generic policies often impose per-item caps that a rare piece exceeds easily. What's needed: an up-to-date professional valuation (renewed periodically, since market values move), detailed photographs of case and movement, and original documentation stored separately from the piece itself.</p>
 <h2>Authorized dealer or private intermediary</h2>
 <p>An authorized dealer operates in the interest of the maison it represents — its priority is the brand relationship, not necessarily the individual client's interest on every transaction. A private intermediary works exclusively for the client, from purchase through sale, with an aligned incentive: we're compensated only if the transaction closes on terms the client approves.</p>
